As a matter of perspective, we might remember that there are a substantial
number of old-world tropical diseases that are extremely vicious, that are
not often encountered in the developed world simply because it has better
sanitation, better medical care, lower population density (sometimes), or
climate unfavorable to the not-in-human phase of the disease organisms,
or unfavorable to the non-human vectors that some of them use.
 
Iraq isn't tropical -- Baghdad has approximately the latitude of Los
Angeles -- but parts of it are warm enough, wet enough (I have in mind
the Tigris/Euphrates/Shatt-al-Arab river country) and close enough to
the true tropics, that there might be cause for worry about naturally
occuring diseases.
 
I'm certainly in no position to say that artificial biological agents were
not encountered; merely that nature has been cooking up weird diseases for
at least as long as has the U. S. Department of Defense.
 
