From: KENHOLDER@delphi.com
Date: Mon, 14 Feb 1994 04:13:08 -0500 (EST)
Subject: [#94-2-135] SCI: Brain Wave Alternation

Kennita asks about experience with what Robert Anton Wilson calls "brain
buzzers."

I have tried several, with varying degrees of success.

Some of the most successful are audio tapes done by Kelly Howell,
self-published by her Brain Sync Corporation, PO Box 9296, Santa Fe, NM
87504. They are usually in stock at various local B. Dalton's book stores.
They must be listened to via headphones to work properly because they use a
"binaural beat" effect -- such as 100 Hz in the left ear and 110 Hz in the
right ear being "heard" by the brain as a 10 Hz sound. Most brain waves are
below 30 Hz or so, and this is a way to make the brain "think" it's hearing
sounds at those frequencies.

The tapes are supposed to work by way of the "frequency following effect" in
which the brain's dominant brain wave frequency tends to mimic that of an
external stimulus, such as drumming, flashing-lights, binaural beats, etc.

The one I use most is "High Focus" by Ms Howell. When it works it allows me
to concentrate on my reading or work with much greater intensity than
normal. It also drowns out distracting sounds. Which is the "real" effect? I
don't know :-). It doesn't work all the time. Sometimes after about 10 or 15
minutes I begin to sweat -- apparently my metabolism has been stepped up a
notch or two! Other times nothing particular happens, I'm not able to
concentrate better, etc. Is it me, or is it Memorex? The tape claims to
increase the level of Beta-waves in the brain. High levels of Beta
activity is associated with alertness, concentration, and such.

Another one I use frequently is her "High-Coherence." This one also works
sometimes and not other times. It claims to get the left & right sides of
the brain working at about the same level of activity instead of the more
normal condition of one side being dominant. It also tries to get a lot of
activity in various Beta and Theta areas. I use it after getting myself
revved-up with "High Focus" because Theta is associated with creativity and
I figure the more of my brain working, the better off I am. I think I am
more creative while programming while listening to this tape, I think. I'm
almost sure, but maybe I'm fooling myself? But then that works too,
sometimes :-).

She has a number of other tapes. I've tried several of them without finding
any especially powerful or useful, although I really like "Brain Massage"
for relaxing, and sometimes use it if "High Focus" gets me too "up." It's
nice for reading, too.

My daughter has used "High Focus" for studying at college and reports it
seems to help her "get into" things, especially when she'd rather do
something else. On the other hand, my best friend reports no effect
whatsoever from any of these tapes. I've urged him to keep trying, but he
doesn't see why he should, since he never got any effect so far.
He's rather "up-tight" and resistant to change, although he claims
otherwise. Other friends have reported finding them enjoyable and useful.
One friend listened to the tape while studying for a final exam (MBA
program) and reported that he drove to work the next morning reviewing
various complex models in economic theory in photographic-memory detail,
much to his amazement; he also refused to return the tape! Obviously
there is a considerable individual variation in response to this stuff.

I also have a light-and-sound machine, the one from Zygon. The
lights-in-the-eyes is supposed to combine with the binaural-
beats-or-percussive-sounds-in-the-ears making a more powerful effect.
However, at least with this machine I can't read or work, so it's less
useful to me. And, of course, sometimes it does seem to really have a
powerful effect an other times it doesn't.

Another problem I've had with it is something Wilson notes somewhere in one
of his reports on the devices. I find the light-machine makes me tense up.
Wilson attributes this to resisting the brain-change the machine is trying
to induce. I *want* the brain change, but apparently resist it anyway. Is it
the old "subconscious" or whatever going its merry way with its own agenda?
I don't know. I had only had the machine a short while and then we moved and
I've not unpacked it to do further experimentation. I did discover that it
takes about an hour of use before I relax or get used to the experience. It
*is* a peculiar thing to do, you know, lie down with bright strobe-lights
pressed against one's closed eyelids and funny noises in the ears! I'm a
neophilus-type person, but I still find this rather excessively far-out!

I've tried to combine the machine with Yogi-type breathing, but find I get
distracted by the light-patterns, which are complex and multi-colored, even
though the two LEDs are red. I've also tried using it with a "talking book"
on tape -- Derek Jacobi reading Robert Fagles' translation of Homer's _The
Iliad_ -- and found the experience quite nice. Talking books usually are too
slow-going and my mind wanders (more than normal I mean). I also tried the
"learn French instantly" tape a few times, but got too busy, so I can't
report on how well that works.

As for other effects, my dreams seemed more vivid and rememberable. My sex
life improved. I got a lot more books read. I really need to start using
them  regularly again; I quit after the move because they were packed and
life was unsettled. In retrospect this seems foolish of me, eh?

So, IN CONCLUSION, the subject needs more research. Try the Kelly Howell
tapes for yourself, they're about $11 or $12. Your mileage may vary. Not
approved by the gummunt for Life Extension or Intelligence Increase. Let us 
know what happens.

