>for example, processes of generation and fragmentation of the
>competing entities.  I claim arguments like Steve's above, predicting
>a single "goo" dominating the future, are suspect for quite similar
>reasons.

The key point is that wealth/power/territory distribution is not a random
walk - what you have affects your ability to get more.  I try to think
of this as a system of differential equations, where each variable grows as a
function of itself, and shrinks as a function of the other variables, and the
total sum is conserved (i.e. system is bounded).  If the growth/shrink function
is a linear function of wealth, then original wealth distributions will be
conserved.  If it is faster-than-linear (ex. x^2), then a single winner
emerges.  If it is slower-than-linear (ex. sqrt(x)), then the final state is
uniform.  Other functions have more interesting behaviors, like several winners
achieving a stable balance.  Anyway, the point is that in the take-over
scenario, the transfer function is primarily faster-than-linear, perhaps only
reaching a plateau after all the (supposedly) limited wealth/power/territory
has been subsumed.  Hence, "winner-takes-all".

Paul


