From: Reilly Jones <70544.1227@CompuServe.COM>
Date: 14 Feb 94 16:23:02 EST
Subject: [#94-2-139] PCR: Creation Ex Nihilo

Steve Witham writes: <PanCritRat is supposed to hold itself open to criticism,
and hold proofs suspect, too (but stick with criticism based on the ideas of
logical consequences and inconsistency, a slightly sticky point).>

Sticking with criticism based on logical consequences and inconsistency is what
leads me to reject creation ex nihilo theories like the Big Bang cosmologies.  I
have previously posted my concerns about the attempt by Robert Nozick in his
1993 book "The Nature of Rationality" to include symbolic utility into the
probabilistic decision value equation.  His reformulation of maximizing decision
value amongst competing courses of action is now:  evidential utility times a
weight plus causal utility times a weight plus symbolic utility times a weight.
The three weights add to 100%.  On the surface, everyone understands that
symbolic utility is real and plays a major role in many types of decisions where
causality and/or evidence is fairly uncertain.  However, I think that rational
decisions should be limited in their definition by constraining decision value
to at least some minimum weight placed on evidential and/or causal utility.
Otherwise, you end up formulating theories of Finality or creation ex nihilo or
pure mysticism all deriving from symbolic utility.  In the case of Big Bang
cosmologies, the symbolic utility is in the form of pure mathematical symbolic
logic with a total absence of evidence or causality.  No one has ever shown that
space-time particles can pop into existence or can disappear from existence nor
has any scientist ever proposed a credible theory of the causality of the Big
Bang (or existence).  Theories that propose space-time particles without quantum
fields or quantum fields without space-time particles or the complete absence of
particles and fields can only be made using symbolic utility alone.  I pronounce
this irrational.  Therefore, something is wrong with a decision value equation
that allows pure symbolic utility to be labelled as rationality.  At least some
measure of evidence and/or causality must be present for rationality to apply.

Dean Tribble wrote: <use a system of rationality such that you can start with
whatever beliefs you currently have an throw out inconsistent ones.> and <When
doing things in the world, act on the basis of the beliefs with the most
credibility.>

This is coherency epistemology.  The weights assigned in the decision value
equation when evaluating various competing beliefs are synonymous with values.
Values are derived from first-person subjective purposes.  Purposes are at the
root of beliefs.

Dean again: <statements that don't make a prediction (or cannot be cast so as to
make a prediction) have little rational content>

I think this is too strongly stated.  Much rational discourse has as its intent,
analysis of the past to sift evidence and establish causality.  Eventually the
results may lead to predictions, but there is no guarantee of this.  In any
case, rationality and predictions certainly are linked as a whole, but not
necessarily in part.

Dean again: <PCR itself is only credible (according to itself) as long as it
continues to bea system for managing beliefs that increase ones ability to
operate in the observable universe. This last point isthe "Pan" part of it: it
applies to itself (and it's not justificational)>

This is a useful statement to help me understand PCR.  Very utilitarian as
indeed Steve Witham wrote, "I think Bartley could be said to justify PCR on the
basis of utility."

Dean again: <PCR does not require that everything be built on axioms that must
then be taken on faith.  Thus, PCR does not require faith in anything.>

What about the axiom "existence exists"?  I have faith in this.  If PCR says we
should doubt this, then PCR could not be rational itself.

Max More wrote: <Belief A may, in fact, be true. It's just that we can never
know for certain whether A is true> and <PCR is motivated by the failure of
every attempt to find foundations for knowledge.> and <PCR says nothing, for
instance, about neuroscience>

Objective reality is true (there really are actual, true structures and motions
of space-time particles and quantum fields on many scales) since there is one
existence (Many-Worlds cosmologies are irrational by PCR standards).  This truth
is what prompts intrinsicist theories.  We can approach the truth of objective
reality through consensual reality using rationality but we cannot be certain of
the truth because we cannot achieve identity with the object.  Consensual
reality is a "knower-and-known" or a "map-and-interpretation" simultaneous whole
at the quantum field level but completely particulate and separated at the
space-time particle level.  Subjective reality, that is, our own thoughts, is
true (not what we are thinking about, but the actual thoughts themselves).  This
truth is what prompts subjectivist theories.  No outsider can know what our
thoughts really are, but can approach the truth of our subjective reality within
consensual reality by using neuroscience.  Using Edelman's "qualia assumption"
which is simply a probabilistic methodology, we can eventually approach very
closely to the truth of subjective reality.  A biological epistemology will
eventually overtake most philosophical epistemologies.

Tim Starr wrote: <PCR would have to solve the problem of the fallibility or
corruptibility of epistemological authorities... by minimizing their fallibility
or corruptibility by saying that epistemological authority is also polycentric>

I see the usefulness here.  This is a good description of the way rationality
becomes defined over time within consensual reality.

Tim Starr again: <we need another term to describe the view that some things are
true in all possible contexts, but need to be grasped through a process of
discovery.>

This "process of discovery" is what I refer to as rationality within consensual
reality.  It's purpose is to approach the truth of both objective reality and
subjective reality.

Tim Starr again: <To be uncertain is to be unable to decide.  To be uncertain of
what one believes is to be unable to decide what one believes.> and <The problem
with intrinsicism is that all knowledge is contextual, and that bad conquences
of acting on a notion is a pretty reliable indicator that there's something
wrong with it.> and <Action, being purposeful behavior, must be guided by some
believed purpose.  If you don't know what you believe your purpose is, then you
can't behave accordingly.  Thought is a necessary condition for action.> and
<This because generating an idea is a kind of human action, and human actions
must be guided by some idea.>

Yes to all of this.  I would add that purpose comes first, then values, which
then are used to assign weight to evidential, causal and symbolic utility in a
rational decision-making process.  Our default purposes are the genetically
coded "homeostatic values" that Edelman refers to, breathing, consumption,
heart-rate, reproduction, etc.  As we grow older, and our social brain is shaped
in consensual reality, we gain the ability to override our default purposes.  We
then can rationally choose to be or not to be, and if to be, how to be.  Thus,
individual first-person subjective purpose is the foundation of all knowledge
that exists between the truths of subjective reality and objective reality.  All
rationality, beliefs and principals are held after values derived from purpose
have been applied to evidence, causality and symbolic meaning.

As Max More posted from the blurb on "The Possibility of Empirical Science" by
David Miller: <The correct response is to kick the justificationist habit
completely, and turn back to the idea that what we seek is the plain unvarnished
truth.>

Yes.  We can approach truth asymptotically forever both outside ourselves and
inside others.

